    },
    {
      "name": "Mobile",
      "value_name": "Mobile",
      "key_paths": [
        "\\{4d36e968-e325-11ce-bfc1-08002be10318}\\"
      ],
      "bit_width": 32,
      "comment": "Type DWORD\nEncoding -- Customer Number\nUsed to determine if Mobile or Desktop and which Mobile Customer"
    },
    {
      "name": "MutuallyExDevs",
      "value_name": "MutuallyExDevs",
      "key_paths": [
        "\\{4d36e968-e325-11ce-bfc1-08002be10318}\\"
      ],
      "bit_width": 32,
      "comment": "Type Binary\nThis is a list of pairs of devmasks.\nEx: (0x00000100, 1), (2, 0x00000200)\nThe reg key for the above example would look like the following\non a little endian x86 machine. The byte ordering would be different\nfor big endian machines.\n00 01 00 00      01 00 00 00      02 00 00 00     00 02 00 00\nThe spaces above are intentional and only serve to separate the devmasks\n(for clarity)\nTo understand the reg key let's assume that reg key = only the first pair\nviz. TV, CRT. This pair tells us that whenever we find a TV with devmask\n0x100, CRT (with devmask 1) can't be present and vice versa. But the\nordering is still important. The pair TV, CRT tells us that we'll\nfirst try to detect TV and only if that is absent will we try CRT.\nEven if someone asks us to detect just the devmask 1 (i.e.\ndoesn't pass in TV's devmask), we'll try TV first and only if that\nis absent will we go ahead with CRT detection. This behaviour is to\nkeep things consistent. For example: If someone asks to detect 0x101\non a Toshiba system (where we assume CRT and TV to be present all the\ntime) with the above reg key in place, we'll return 0x100 as the connected\nmask. Now if someone asks to detect just 1, we can't directly go and\ndetect CRT because if we were to do so, we'll return CRT as connected\nsince it's assumed to be connected all the time. Return 0x100 in one case\nand 1 in the other is inconsistent. We must return 0 for the latter."
    },
    {
      "name": "MXMData",
      "value_name": "MXMData",
      "key_paths": [
        "\\{4d36e968-e325-11ce-bfc1-08002be10318}\\"
      ],
      "bit_width": 32,
      "comment": "Type BINARY\nWhole MXM Structure in the registry key"
    },
    {
      "name": "MXMDisable",
      "value_name": "MXMDisable",
      "key_paths": [
        "\\{4d36e968-e325-11ce-bfc1-08002be10318}\\"
      ],
      "bit_width": 32,
      "comment": "Type Dword\nIf set, it will disable MXM in RM even if it is a MXM system",
      "fields": [
        {
          "name": "MXMDisable",
          "bits": [0],
          "states": [
            { "value": 0, "name": "DEFAULT" },
            { "value": 1, "name": "ENABLE" }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "MXMSize",
      "value_name": "MXMSize",
      "key_paths": [
        "\\{4d36e968-e325-11ce-bfc1-08002be10318}\\"
      ],
      "bit_width": 32,
      "comment": "Type BINARY\nOverrides the Size of the MXM structure in bytes"
    },
    {
      "name": "MXMVersion",
      "value_name": "MXMVersion",
      "key_paths": [
        "\\{4d36e968-e325-11ce-bfc1-08002be10318}\\"
      ],
      "bit_width": 32,
      "comment": "Type BINARY\nFor overriding MXM Version"
    },
    {
      "name": "nvBrokenFb",
      "value_name": "nvBrokenFb",
      "key_paths": [
        "\\{4d36e968-e325-11ce-bfc1-08002be10318}\\"
      ],
      "bit_width": 32,
      "comment": "Type DWORD\n_ALL_OKAY:       FB is not broken.  All is okay.\n_ALL_BROKEN:     FB is broken and no softwre will try to use it.\n_MEMORY:         Memory itself can/cannot be accessed.  (PDB_PROP_GPU_BROKEN_FB property)\n_REG_VIA_CPU:    CPU can/cannot access FBPA/FBIO registers.  (PDB_PROP_GPU_BROKEN_FB_REG_VIA_CPU property)\n_REG_VIA_PMU:    PMU can/cannot access FBPA/FBIO registers.  (PDB_PROP_GPU_BROKEN_FB_REG_VIA_PMU property)\nFBPA/FBIO register addresses are defined by gpuIsBrokenFbReg().\nNote that if the CPU and the PMU can't access registers, then memory isn't going to work either.\nIn other words, the only even number that makes sense for this regkey is zero.\nDefault depends on the chip and mask revision.",
      "fields": [
        {
          "name": "MEMORY",
          "bits": [0],
          "states": [
            { "value": 0, "name": "OKAY" },
            { "value": 1, "name": "BROKEN" }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "REG_VIA_CPU",
          "bits": [1],
          "states": [
            { "value": 0, "name": "OKAY" },
            { "value": 1, "name": "BROKEN" }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "REG_VIA_PMU",
          "bits": [2],
          "states": [
            { "value": 0, "name": "OKAY" },
            { "value": 1, "name": "BROKEN" }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "NvLinkPeerThroughL2",
      "value_name": "NvLinkPeerThroughL2",
      "key_paths": [
        "\\{4d36e968-e325-11ce-bfc1-08002be10318}\\"
      ],
      "bit_width": 32,
      "comment": "TYPE DWORD\nThis regkey manually enables or disabled caching of peer traffic in L2.\n0 - DEFAULT behaviour i.e. ENABLED for SLI config (Volta+) & DISABLED\nfor non-SLI config\n1 - force ENABLED\n2 - force DISABLED\nNote: Enabling this will cause degraded performance for compute and improved\nperformance for things such as AFR rendering in GeForce.",
      "fields": [
        {
          "name": "NvLinkPeerThroughL2",
          "bits": [0, 1],
          "states": [
            { "value": 0, "name": "DEFAULT" },
            { "value": 1, "name": "ENABLED" },
            { "value": 2, "name": "DISABLED" }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "OverDriveData0",
      "value_name": "OverDriveData0",
      "key_paths": [
        "\\{4d36e968-e325-11ce-bfc1-08002be10318}\\"
      ],
      "bit_width": 32,
      "comment": "Type Binary\n0 will be replaced with the HEX value (0-F) of the current panel strap when reading this regkey.\nThe binary format is known to the video group.  For details on the issue, see bug 135902.\nThis regkey is also used for defining overdrive data for EDID product code (OverdrivePanelProductCode0)"
    },
    {
      "name": "OverDriveDataSerialNo0",
      "value_name": "OverDriveDataSerialNo0",
      "key_paths": [
        "\\{4d36e968-e325-11ce-bfc1-08002be10318}\\"
      ],
      "bit_width": 32,
      "comment": "Type Binary\n0 will be replaced with the HEX value (0-F) of the index defined by OverdrivePanelSerialNo0 when\nreading this regkey.\nThe binary format is known to the video group.  For details on the issue, see bug 135902."
    },
    {
      "name": "OverdrivePanelProductCode0",
      "value_name": "OverdrivePanelProductCode0",
      "key_paths": [
        "\\{4d36e968-e325-11ce-bfc1-08002be10318}\\"
      ],
      "bit_width": 32,
      "comment": "Type DWORD\nOverdrivePanelProductCodeX: define product code for overdrive data index X (serial = 0)"
    },
    {
      "name": "OverdrivePanelSerialNo0",
      "value_name": "OverdrivePanelSerialNo0",
      "key_paths": [
        "\\{4d36e968-e325-11ce-bfc1-08002be10318}\\"
      ],
      "bit_width": 32,
      "comment": "Type DWORD\nOverdrivePanelSerialNoXY define serial number for overdriver serial data index X"
    },
    {
      "name": "OverrideEdidFlags\\0\\0",
      "value_name": "OverrideEdidFlags\\0\\0",
      "key_paths": [
        "\\{4d36e968-e325-11ce-bfc1-08002be10318}\\"
      ],
      "bit_width": 32,
      "comment": "Type BINARY\nEncoding:\nThese bytes allow specific, special handling for bad EDIDs.\nThat is, if a particular EDID matches the manufacturer ID, the Product ID,\nand the EDID date falls between the Years (inclusive) and between the weeks if the years\nmatch (inclusive), then we'll override specific ideas stored in the EDID based on the\nFlags bits.  This registry key should only be used as a backup to add support\nfor a released driver via the INF. Otherwise, this data should be hardcoded in the RM's\nfunction, initDacRegistryOverrides().\nSince more than one of these can be defined, I've added \\0\\0 at the end so\nthat we can use strings like \"OverrideEdidFlags0\", \"OverrideEdidFlags1\", ....\nBinary Structure:         Description                                  Byte combines\nNvU16 ManufacturerID; // EDID Manufacturer ID -> offset 0x8 in EDID.  Byte[0] | (Byte[1]<<8)\nNvU16 ProductID;      // EDID Product ID -> offset 0xA in EDID        Byte[2] | (Byte[3]<<8)\nNvU16 MinYearWeek;    // Internal min/max yearweek test (inclusive)   Byte[4] | (Byte[5]<<8)\nNvU16 MaxYearWeek     // Year in EDID found at offset 0x11            Byte[6] | (Byte[7]<<8)\n// Week in EDID found at offset 0x10\nNvU32 Flags;          // Flags that describe override properties      Byte[8] | (Byte[9]<<8) | (Byte[10]<<16) | (Byte[11]<<24)\nNvU32 OverrideStart;  // First byte of EDID to override with EDIDdata Byte[12]\nNvU32 OverrideSize;   // Number of bytes to override with EDIDdata    Byte[13]\nNvU8 EDIDdata[256];   // 1-256 bytes of data to overwrite part of EDIDByte[14] - Byte[14+255]\n// (checksum is updated automatically)\nFlags definitions:\nDAC_EDID_NVFLAG_ANALOG_LOAD_TEST  1\nIf this bit is set, we should always test the analog load, regardless of whether or\nnot the EDID reports a digital or analog device to determine if the edid is valid\n(digital vs. analog found in the edid at offset 0x14, bit 7)\nDAC_EDID_NVFLAG_ALLOW_CVT_RB_TIMINGS  NVBIT(1)\nIf this bit is set anywhere in the EDID override flags, then if we match the EDID\nthen we'll allow CVT timings to be used.  If we don't match the EDID we'll disallow\nCVT Timigns to be used.  If the flag is absent, then we allow CVT timings for all\nEDIDs.\nDAC_EDID_NVFLAG_OVERWRITE_EDID_BLOCK  NVBIT(2)\nIf this bit is set, later data indicates the start and size of a section of the EDID\nto override, and the data to override with.\nIf ManufacturerID is 0, then the code assumes that it's not there.  The RM will read\nOverrideEdidFlags0 first.  If it's not found, it will stop looking for keys. If it\nis found, then it will continue with OverrideEdidFlags1, etc.."
    },
    {
      "name": "OverrideFbSize",
